# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY TRACKED, so that a later edit has to argue with a
# sentence before it can drop it:
#
#   results/*.jsonl  THE EVIDENCE. The README.md figures that
#                    scripts/check_readme_numbers.py lists are derived from
#                    these, and CI fails if one stops matching. THREE runs
#                    of 1,860 rows -- sweep.jsonl and sweep_openai.jsonl, one
#                    per model vendor, plus sweep_replicate.jsonl, a second
#                    run of the Claude configuration that is the only thing
#                    here measuring run-to-run variance.
#                    sweep.failures.jsonl is the retry log and is empty,
#                    which is the claim it makes.
#   cqa/             the world, the questions, the ten conditions, the grader
#                    and the governance detector
#   scripts/         the pre-flight gate, the runner, the reporter and the
#                    figure deriver
#   tests/           the suite, including the mutation-checked guards
#
# THE RESULTS ARE THE POINT AND THEY ARE SMALL. About 4.3 MB of JSONL
# holds every generation: the answer, the per-field grade, the governance
# verdict, token usage, and a hash of the question set and of the condition
# that produced the row. Without them nothing in README.md can be
# checked by a reader, which is the whole reason they ship.
#
# Excluded below is only what a clone can rebuild.

envs
.venv
__pycache__
.pytest_cache
*.pyc
.DS_Store

# The default credential path. scripts/sweep.py reads the key from the file
# ENV_FILE names and falls back to ./.env when ENV_FILE is unset, so an
# operator who follows the shortest path leaves a key file in the working
# tree. Untracked is not the same as ignored: an untracked .env shows up in
# `git status` and is picked up by `git add -A`. SECURITY.md says credentials
# are never committed, and this line is what makes that true.
.env
.env.*
# The template ships; it holds no value.
!.env.example

# scripts/mutation_suite.py's crash-recovery state. The sentinel names the file
# it has patched and the pristine copy is what puts it back, so both must
# survive a killed run and neither may be committed: a pristine copy in a
# commit is a second copy of a source file, and a sentinel in a commit tells
# the next reader the tree is mid-patch when it is not.
.mutation_suite_state.json
.mutation_suite_pristine/
